Reset the cached TIA-surface pointer when the console is destroyed.

getVideoBuffer() memoizes the console's TIA-surface base pointer in the
StellaLIBRETRO member render_surface (fetched once, on first call). The
StellaLIBRETRO object is global and reused across game launches, so a frontend
that keeps the core loaded and relaunches via retro_load_game without a fresh
retro_init (e.g. REG-Station's in-process Libra player) never re-fetches it:
after destroy()/create() builds a new FrameBuffer + TIA surface, render_surface
still points at the previous, now-freed surface. The emulation then runs fine
on the new console (RAM/TIA advance) but the frontend uploads the stale surface
every frame, so video is frozen on the 2nd+ launch. Process-per-game frontends
never hit this.

Clear render_surface in destroy() so getVideoBuffer() re-fetches the live
surface for the next console.

--- a/src/os/libretro/StellaLIBRETRO.cxx
+++ b/src/os/libretro/StellaLIBRETRO.cxx
@@ -121,6 +121,14 @@
   video_ready = false;
   audio_samples = 0;
 
+  // Invalidate the cached TIA-surface pointer: getVideoBuffer() memoizes it
+  // in render_surface, and myOSystem.reset() below frees the surface it points
+  // to. Frontends that reuse this (global) object across game launches without
+  // a fresh retro_init (e.g. REG-Station's in-process Libra player) would
+  // otherwise keep uploading the previous, now-stale surface every frame — the
+  // emulation runs but the video is frozen on the 2nd+ launch.
+  render_surface = nullptr;
+
   myOSystem.reset();
 }
